      "id": 1,
      "text": "Needham’s first post office in 1905 had to drop the original name Bogueloosa because another Louisiana town already used it.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/needham/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Before incorporation, Needham’s economy was dominated by cotton plantations that spread across the land the town now occupies.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/needham/",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In the 1930s, Needham struggled, and the New Deal brought relief through a commodity distribution store near the railroad tracks that supplied food and clothing to workers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://needhamlocal.org/2024/09/needham-history-the-new-deal-in-needham/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Needham appears on La Tourette’s 1844 map of Alabama, making it one of the earliest documented settlements in the state.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/needham/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Needham was officially incorporated in December 1981, a surprisingly recent date for a community with deep roots.",
